  "components": [
    {
      "name": "Perceived Restorativeness Scale (PRS)",
      "description": "A 16-item self-report scale designed to measure the restorative quality of environments. Respondents rate statements on a 7-point scale (0 = Not at all, 6 = Completely). Items are theoretically grouped into four a priori subscales: Being Away, Fascination, Coherence (Extent), and Compatibility. Empirically, a 2-factor solution is often recommended, combining Being Away, Fascination, and Compatibility into a 'General Restorativeness' subscale, and retaining Coherence as a separate subscale (after item reversal).",
      "validation": "Validated across four studies for internal consistency, convergent, discriminant, and criterion validity, and sensitivity to environmental differences. Consistent across different populations and presentation modes."
    },
    {
      "name": "Being Away subscale",
      "description": "2 items: 'It is an escape experience.', 'Spending time here gives me a good break from my day-to-day routine.' Intended to measure the sense of getting distance from routine aspects of life.",
      "validation": "Generally sufficient internal consistency, but empirically loads with Fascination and Compatibility onto a 'General Restorativeness' factor."
    },
    {
      "name": "Fascination subscale",
      "description": "5 items (one replaced in Study 2): 'The setting has fascinating qualities.', 'My attention is drawn to many interesting things.', 'I would like to get to know this place better.', 'I want to explore the area.' (replaced by 'There is much to explore and discover here.' in Study 2), 'I would like to spend more time looking at the surroundings.' Intended to measure effortless attention drawn to interesting stimuli.",
      "validation": "Generally sufficient internal consistency, but empirically loads with Being Away and Compatibility onto a 'General Restorativeness' factor."
    },
    {
      "name": "Coherence (Extent) subscale",
      "description": "4 negatively worded items: 'There is too much going on.', 'It is a confusing place.', 'There is a great deal of distraction.', 'It is chaotic here.' Intended to measure the ease with which one can organize and structure a scene, reflecting connectedness and scope. Items are reversed for scoring.",
      "validation": "Often characterized by low internal consistency in some sites. Empirically defines a distinct factor from other PRS items. Shows differential sensitivity to site characteristics, supporting construct validity despite reliability issues."
    },
    {
      "name": "Compatibility subscale",
      "description": "5 items: 'I can do things I like here.', 'I have a sense that I belong here.', 'I have a sense of oneness with this setting.', 'Being here suits my personality.', 'I could find ways to enjoy myself in a place like this.' Intended to measure the match between personal goals/inclinations and environmental support for activities.",
      "validation": "Generally sufficient internal consistency, but empirically loads with Being Away and Fascination onto a 'General Restorativeness' factor."
    },
    {
      "name": "General Restorativeness subscale (empirical)",
      "description": "A composite score formed by combining the a priori Being Away, Fascination, and Compatibility items, based on empirical factor analysis results. Represents a broad sense of restorativeness.",
      "validation": "Demonstrated good internal consistency and strong correlations with positive affect and environmental characteristics across studies."
    }
  ],
  "psychometric_properties": {
    "reliability": "Internal consistency (Cronbach's alpha > .75) for Being Away, Fascination, and Compatibility subscales generally sufficient across sites and studies. Coherence subscale sometimes showed insufficient reliability.",
    "validity": "Factorial validity: A priori 4-factor structure (Being Away, Fascination, Extent, Compatibility) was not stable; empirical analyses consistently suggested a 2-factor solution (General Restorativeness and Coherence). Convergent validity: General PRS correlated positively with KSS Originality; Coherence PRS correlated negatively with KSS Complexity. Discriminant validity: Few significant correlations with KSS Affection, Potency, and Social Status. Criterion validity: General PRS correlated positively with ZIPERS Positive Affect and Attentiveness, and Coherence PRS correlated negatively with ZIPERS Sadness, Fear Arousal, and Anger/Aggression. Sensitivity: PRS scores differentiated between natural vs. built, outdoor vs. indoor, and high vs. low restorative potential sites in theoretically meaningful ways across all four studies."
  },
  "recommended_use": "To represent core constructs of Attention Restoration Theory (ART), distinguish between environments differing in restorative potential, assess restorative potential of existing and proposed settings, and inform design efforts. For practical use, authors suggest combining Being Away, Fascination, and Compatibility items into a 'General Restorativeness' subscale, alongside the Coherence subscale, unless specific factor analysis indicates otherwise.",
  "domains": [
    "A3_Spatial_Config",
    "A4_Light",
    "A6_Visual_Form",
    "A8_Social",
    "A9_Task_Cognition",
    "A10_Temporal"
  ],
  "overall_theory_links": [
    "ART"
  ],
  "limitations": [
    "Factorial validity: The a priori 4-factor structure (Being Away, Fascination, Compatibility, Extent) was not consistently supported by factor analyses, which instead suggested a 2-factor solution (General Restorativeness and Coherence).",
    "Theoretical vs. empirical factor structure: The empirical grouping of Being Away, Fascination, and Compatibility into a single 'General' factor raises questions about their theoretical independence, though authors suggest maintaining theoretical distinction for further research.",
    "Sample size for factor analysis: Limited samples of individuals, environments, and items in factor analyses may have contributed to the observed factor structure.",
    "Item wording issues: Some item wording (e.g., 'good' in a Being Away item) might inadvertently conflate different theoretical constructs, contributing to the empirical factor structure.",
    "Extent construct representation: The domain of possible extent items was not adequately represented in the PRS, with retained items primarily focusing on 'coherence' in negative terms.",
    "Coherence subscale reliability: The Coherence subscale was more often characterized by low internal consistency compared to other subscales.",
    "Negative wording of Coherence items: All Coherence items are negatively worded, which might influence their factor loading and interpretation.",
    "Simulation vs. actual experience: While evaluations obtained via simulations were similar to on-site evaluations, this does not confirm that simulations provide the same restorative effects as actual environments, as simulations may limit interaction and mute restorativeness or exclude unpleasant qualities."
  ],
